---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';

const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-attentive';
const title = 'Drip vs Attentive for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = "Attentive now offers email as well as SMS. A Shopify team considering a move from Drip should compare a complete Attentive email-and-text program with keeping Drip email and coordinating a separate text service. Revenue size alone does not settle that architecture decision.";

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Attentive offer email as well as SMS?",
    "answer": "Yes. Attentive markets email journeys and cross-channel coordination. Evaluate the available package rather than treating it as an SMS-only service."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Attentive replace Drip for this store?",
    "answer": "Potentially, if the purchased email product reproduces the required journeys, exclusions and migration needs. This page proposes checks; it does not report a completed migration."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I keep Drip alongside Attentive?",
    "answer": "That can preserve existing email workflows, but it introduces a synchronization boundary that needs explicit ownership and failure handling."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare costs?",
    "answer": "Compare matching email and text volumes, support scope and implementation labor using current quotes. Store revenue is not a pricing formula."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs Attentive for Shopify</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Attentive now offers email as well as SMS. A Shopify team considering a move from Drip should compare a complete Attentive email-and-text program with keeping Drip email and coordinating a separate text service. Revenue size alone does not settle that architecture decision.</p></header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Consolidating email and SMS without losing control</h2>
    <p>This comparison is an editorial evaluation framework, checked against primary product information on 24 November 2026. The exercises below are proposed tests; no merchant trial or performance result is claimed.</p>
    <h2>Email is part of the decision</h2>
    <p>Attentive markets cross-channel journeys, a no-code builder and email migration assistance. The old assumption that it necessarily needs another email provider is incorrect. Drip remains an email automation candidate; selecting it does not establish that any particular SMS service or consent transfer is included. Map the channels in your actual contract before comparing costs.</p>
    <h2>Proposed rehearsal: VIP launch with two permissions</h2>
    <p>Use a hypothetical apparel launch with three test customers: one subscribed to email only, one to SMS only, and one to both. Start with an early-access invitation, then a public launch reminder. Write the expected message for each identity before building. In Attentive, ask the demonstrator to show channel eligibility and the shared journey. In Drip, build the email path and identify the exact mechanism that would tell a separate SMS service to exclude a purchaser. A customer tag is a segment signal, not evidence of text consent.</p>
    <h2>Purchase during the delay</h2>
    <p>Place a controlled order after the invitation but before the reminder. Inspect the actual customer record and queued steps; do not infer suppression from an order appearing on a dashboard. Repeat with the same email but a changed phone number, then with email unsubscribed while SMS remains eligible. Log timestamps and destinations. Decide whether a channel preference change removes only the relevant step or exits the entire journey. These are proposed acceptance checks, not measured vendor results.</p>
    <h2>Welcome, cart and post-purchase coverage</h2>
    <p>Recreate the email welcome sequence before retiring Drip, including signup-source branches, incentive eligibility and exclusions for existing customers. For cart recovery, inspect the checkout URL and product fallback when stock changes. For post-purchase education, separate an order confirmation from marketing; determine which system owns each message. A replacement is ready only when the team can account for these existing jobs, including workflows that are less visible than launch broadcasts.</p>
    <h2>One platform or two operational owners</h2>
    <p>Consolidation can remove a synchronization boundary, but only if the new platform expresses the required eligibility and exit rules. Keeping Drip can preserve existing email logic while an SMS specialist serves text. That route needs an owner for data transfer failures, incident response and cross-channel exclusions. Ask who can pause all promotional sends when an inventory problem occurs. Test that action using internal recipients rather than assuming a spreadsheet synchronizes live queues.</p>
    <h2>Migration scope and support</h2>
    <p>Attentive advertises email migration support. Obtain a written inventory of templates, journeys, historical suppression records and authentication tasks included in your proposal. Require a handover in which your own operator changes the VIP exclusion and explains the result. Assistance has practical value when it transfers working knowledge; it does not prove future conversion or inbox placement. Keep Drip automation paused at the agreed cutover boundary and retain a rollback record.</p>
    <h2>Compare the same launch calendar</h2>
    <p>Request an Attentive quote covering email, SMS destinations, message types, support, renewal and seasonal use. Use the Drip calculator for the actual active-person count and send volume, then add the separate text service and integration maintenance if retaining two tools. Compare ordinary months and launch months. Do not treat attributed SMS sales as incremental profit: email and text may both claim the same order.</p>
    <h2>Decision record</h2>
    <p>Choose Attentive consolidation when its available email journeys meet your existing requirements and joint channel operation is easier for the team to maintain. Retain Drip when its email setup continues to fit and the separate text boundary is demonstrably reliable. Leave the decision open if identity merging, regional channel availability or the support scope cannot be demonstrated in your account. There is no universal SMS revenue percentage at which a switch becomes necessary.</p>
    <h2>Primary sources and limits</h2><ul>
      <li><a class="link" href="https://www.attentive.com/email-marketing">Attentive email product scope</a></li>
      <li><a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424695491725-Connect-Your-Shopify-Store">Drip Shopify connection</a></li>
      <li><a class="link" href="https://www.drip.com/pricing">Drip current pricing</a></li>
    </ul><p>Product documentation establishes advertised scope. Account access, integration latency and commercial terms still require confirmation for the proposed setup. No runtime tests were performed for this comparison.</p>
    </article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-attentive">Omnisend vs Attentive</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/fashion">Email apps for fashion</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/black-friday">Black Friday playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs Attentive FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
